  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Before summer 2026, Saint-Raphaël's supervised beaches were off-limits to dogs in the bathing season, even on a leash. For summer 2026, the town opened a "caniplage" on Beaurivage beach, next to the Diana jetty, right in the town centre; Nice-Matin (17 July 2026) described it as accessible until 30 September.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The town of Saint-Raphaël published an announcement on 13 July 2026 titled "A Beaurivage, les chiens ont désormais leur plage" ("At Beaurivage, dogs now have their beach"): until 30 September, a portion of the beach, between the Diana jetty and the breakwater next to the lifeguard station, is dedicated to dogs. Estérel Côte d'Azur also lists Beau Rivage among its beaches for dogs, while the supervised beaches and several other sites in the commune remain off-limits to dogs in the bathing season. The 2026 season ended on 30 September.</p>
      <h2>The rules announced by the town</h2>
      <p>Dogs must be identified, remain under the constant supervision of an adult and be kept on a leash on the access paths and across the whole beach, except while swimming, when they may be briefly unleashed right next to their owner. The owner must be able to recall their dog at any time and pick up waste; a bag dispenser is installed at the beach entrance. First- and second-category dogs, as well as those subject to an administrative or judicial measure restricting their movement or showing dangerous behaviour, are not allowed.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: to find out whether the arrangement will be renewed in 2027, call the town hall (04 94 82 15 00). In the meantime, Plage du Pacha in Fréjus, cited by Nice-Matin as an alternative, is a neighbouring dog beach.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Beau Rivage is one of Saint-Raphaël's supervised beaches, along with Le Veillat, Agay, Le Lido, La Péguière and Le Dramont: outside the dedicated portion, these beaches are off-limits to dogs in the bathing season, even on a leash, except assistance dogs (the town's Animal Guide). The nearby Jardin Beaurivage is home to one of the commune's five "cani-sites", a developed space where dogs can relieve themselves freely; the others are on boulevard Saint-Exupéry, at the jardin des Orangers in Santa Lucia, at Boulouris and at Agay. The town centre is steps away.</p>
    </div>

